1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,090
Open Uber. See those little
cars around you?

2
00:00:03,140 --> 00:00:07,050
To find you a driver,
Uber cut the planet into hexagons.

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:09,560
Today, we're following one
of those little cars,

4
00:00:09,610 --> 00:00:11,850
from a driver's phone
all the way to yours.

5
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:15,650
Along the way, its location goes through
a server bombarded with questions,

6
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:18,470
and a GPS that's off by 50 meters.

7
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:22,710
And in 2015, two researchers
asked an awkward question.

8
00:00:22,830 --> 00:00:25,050
Do all those cars
actually exist?

9
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:30,090
I've got a buddy who reads
big companies' tech blogs,

10
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:33,370
to see how they build
products used by millions

11
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:35,710
of people. One day,
he sends me an article from Uber,

12
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:38,170
about something called polling.

13
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:41,210
To him, Uber's engineers
are geniuses.

14
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:43,190
Me? Maps with
stuff on them,

15
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:45,150
I've displayed those
in my Flutter apps,

16
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,990
but never with millions
of people at the same time,

17
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:51,890
and in real time. So I read
his article, then the others.

18
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:54,630
At the end, I'll tell you whether
my buddy was right or not.

19
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:59,910
So, Uber is over 40 million
trips and deliveries a day.

20
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:03,730
Let's take just one trip.
Put yourself in the shoes of a driver

21
00:01:03,780 --> 00:01:07,430
in Paris: his phone sends
his location, and it has to reach

22
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:11,070
your screen, two streets away.
Let's follow that location.

23
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:16,310
Uber gets the locations of millions
of drivers. You want

24
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:19,110
a handful: the ones around you.

25
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:21,950
The naive solution is to
calculate the distance between you

26
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,370
and every driver. Which means
a million drivers

27
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:28,110
gives you a million calculations
for just one person.

28
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:31,690
And people opening the Uber app
at the same moment? There are millions.

29
00:01:31,740 --> 00:01:36,030
It's like hunting for Mewtwo
in 2016 in Pokémon Go.

30
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:42,030
The trick is to sort things.
You cut the map into cells,

31
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:45,550
and each driver goes into their own.
When you open the app,

32
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:48,210
Uber checks your cell
and the ones next to it.

33
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:52,230
The obvious move is to use squares.
Look at the grid for two seconds.

34
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:53,350
See the flaw?

35
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:58,320
Your cell has four neighbors
touching it by a side,

36
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:03,280
and four touching by a corner.
The corner ones are 41% farther.

37
00:02:03,810 --> 00:02:07,640
So the cell next door could mean
really close… or way farther.

38
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:11,560
But if you take a hexagon,
it has six neighbors.

39
00:02:11,610 --> 00:02:13,720
All of its neighbors
are the same distance away.

40
00:02:14,010 --> 00:02:18,860
In 2018, Uber open-sources
its hexagonal grid. It's called H3.

41
00:02:18,910 --> 00:02:22,640
You give H3 a GPS location,
and it gives you back your hexagon.

42
00:02:22,690 --> 00:02:25,710
Add one ring around it:
seven cells. Two rings: nineteen.

43
00:02:25,930 --> 00:02:28,300
Devs call that a k-ring.

44
00:02:28,830 --> 00:02:30,580
So instead of
a million drivers,

45
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:34,220
Uber only goes through about a hundred:
the ones in those nineteen cells.

46
00:02:34,910 --> 00:02:38,320
If you like understanding what's
hiding behind the apps you've got

47
00:02:38,370 --> 00:02:42,900
in your pocket, subscribe
to my channel: we open them one by one.

48
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:47,540
Seven cells is enough to find
our rare trainer or our shiny Pokémon.

49
00:02:47,810 --> 00:02:50,980
But to set a price,
Uber compares, hexagon by hexagon,

50
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:54,420
ride requests and available drivers
across the whole city.

51
00:02:54,470 --> 00:02:57,700
That famous 1.8x surge
on a Saturday at 2 a.m.?

52
00:02:57,750 --> 00:02:59,600
Your hexagon made that call.

53
00:03:00,430 --> 00:03:03,500
So H3 has 16 zoom levels,
and at each level,

54
00:03:03,550 --> 00:03:06,480
the hexagons are seven times
smaller. At the widest,

55
00:03:06,530 --> 00:03:09,460
a hexagon is about eight
times the size of France. At the finest,

56
00:03:09,510 --> 00:03:10,960
less than a square meter.

57
00:03:11,630 --> 00:03:14,880
Well, we say hexagons,
but they're almost hexagons.

58
00:03:14,930 --> 00:03:18,800
You can't cover a ball
entirely with hexagons:

59
00:03:18,850 --> 00:03:20,440
you have to cheat somewhere.

60
00:03:21,050 --> 00:03:24,220
So H3 hides twelve
pentagons at every level.

61
00:03:24,410 --> 00:03:27,140
And so they don't land
in the middle of a city,

62
00:03:27,190 --> 00:03:30,980
the grid is oriented
to put them all in the ocean.

63
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:32,420
Somewhere in the middle of the water,

64
00:03:32,470 --> 00:03:36,100
there are twelve five-sided cells
where no Uber will ever go.

65
00:03:36,890 --> 00:03:39,800
Uber now knows which
drivers are around you. What's left

66
00:03:39,850 --> 00:03:44,500
is sending them to you, live.
And for years, Uber got that wrong.

67
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:49,780
Because at first, the Uber app
acted like a kid in the back

68
00:03:49,830 --> 00:03:53,120
of the car. Over and over, he'd ask:
"Are we there yet?

69
00:03:53,170 --> 00:03:55,560
Are we there yet?
Are we there yet?"

70
00:03:58,530 --> 00:04:00,980
Every few seconds,
your phone asked the server:

71
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:03,840
"Anything new?" And most
of the time, it said no.

72
00:04:03,990 --> 00:04:08,380
That's called polling, and that's
what my buddy's article was about.

73
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:13,320
But every "no" costs battery
and puts work on the server.

74
00:04:13,690 --> 00:04:17,620
In that 2020 article,
Uber gives the numbers: at one point,

75
00:04:17,670 --> 00:04:18,680
eight out of ten requests received

76
00:04:18,730 --> 00:04:22,060
at the front door, meaning
the API gateway, were polling.

77
00:04:22,130 --> 00:04:26,480
And on a spotty 2G or 3G network,
every failure fired off more requests.

78
00:04:26,530 --> 00:04:30,800
If you've ever stared at an app
spinning with one bar of signal,

79
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:32,220
you get the picture.

80
00:04:32,430 --> 00:04:36,540
The fix? Flip the conversation.
The phone stops asking,

81
00:04:36,590 --> 00:04:40,160
and the server speaks up
when there's something new. This time,

82
00:04:40,210 --> 00:04:43,780
it's called push. For that,
Uber built a RAMEN.

83
00:04:43,850 --> 00:04:47,320
No, not the noodles.
And excuse my English accent:

84
00:04:47,370 --> 00:04:49,640
it's Realtime Asynchronous
Messaging Network.

85
00:04:49,770 --> 00:04:52,260
A real-time messaging network.

86
00:04:52,310 --> 00:04:55,600
Honestly, I'm pretty sure they
came up with the name first. RAMEN

87
00:04:55,650 --> 00:04:58,780
has three parts. To get them,
take Uber Eats.

88
00:04:59,270 --> 00:05:02,000
Fireball is the manager.
It sees every event go by:

89
00:05:02,050 --> 00:05:05,660
a rider orders,
a driver accepts, a car moves.

90
00:05:05,710 --> 00:05:08,460
And for each one, it decides who
needs to be told, and when.

91
00:05:08,510 --> 00:05:11,640
The API gateway is the kitchen.
It prepares the order:

92
00:05:11,690 --> 00:05:15,060
all the data the app
needs, in the right format.

93
00:05:15,310 --> 00:05:18,960
And the RAMEN server, not the Ichiraku
one, of course, is the delivery guy.

94
00:05:19,010 --> 00:05:21,580
It keeps a connection
open with your phone,

95
00:05:21,630 --> 00:05:24,420
and delivers messages with a promise:

96
00:05:24,930 --> 00:05:27,260
every message arrives at least once.

97
00:05:27,390 --> 00:05:30,620
So our driver's location
reaches you live,

98
00:05:30,670 --> 00:05:33,600
without draining your battery.
But this whole time, I've been acting

99
00:05:33,650 --> 00:05:35,500
as if that location was accurate.

100
00:05:38,050 --> 00:05:39,900
In a city, it can be
off by 50 meters.

101
00:05:39,950 --> 00:05:42,220
Uber's engineers
say so themselves,

102
00:05:42,270 --> 00:05:46,400
and sometimes it's worse. 50 meters
is the other side of the boulevard. On

103
00:05:46,450 --> 00:05:50,060
your map, the driver waits at the station.
In real life, he's parked across from you.

104
00:05:50,270 --> 00:05:53,400
If the app slid the cars
from one location to the next,

105
00:05:53,450 --> 00:05:57,380
the driver would show up like in
Fast and Furious, full throttle. Drifting,

106
00:05:57,430 --> 00:06:00,960
straight through buildings. A 50-meter
jump in one second, on screen,

107
00:06:01,010 --> 00:06:02,700
that's 180 km/h.

108
00:06:04,030 --> 00:06:06,540
So why is the GPS
that far off?

109
00:06:06,590 --> 00:06:09,920
The driver's phone works out its
location from the time it takes

110
00:06:09,970 --> 00:06:12,060
for satellite signals to reach it.

111
00:06:12,110 --> 00:06:14,660
But cities have buildings.
A signal hits

112
00:06:14,710 --> 00:06:17,220
a facade, bounces off, and arrives late.

113
00:06:17,710 --> 00:06:20,300
The phone thinks the satellite
is farther away than it really is,

114
00:06:20,350 --> 00:06:22,300
and puts itself in the wrong spot.

115
00:06:22,450 --> 00:06:25,720
In 2018, Uber engineers
had a wild idea:

116
00:06:25,770 --> 00:06:28,280
use the problem as a clue.

117
00:06:28,330 --> 00:06:31,800
On Android, the phone knows the
signal strength of every satellite.

118
00:06:31,930 --> 00:06:34,700
Weak signal?
There's probably a building between it

119
00:06:34,750 --> 00:06:37,020
and the satellite. Strong signal?
The path is clear.

120
00:06:37,070 --> 00:06:38,800
Compare that to a 3D map of the city,

121
00:06:38,850 --> 00:06:44,000
and you can tell which side
of the street the driver is on.

122
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:47,020
They find their way using
the buildings' shadows.

123
00:06:47,070 --> 00:06:50,380
And to track the car,
they scatter thousands

124
00:06:50,430 --> 00:06:52,960
of possible cars through the streets.
At each measurement, the ones that match

125
00:06:53,010 --> 00:06:56,340
the signals stay,
the others vanish. That's a particle

126
00:06:56,390 --> 00:06:58,540
filter, tested in San Francisco.

127
00:06:59,270 --> 00:07:00,620
Quick aside for the devs:

128
00:07:00,670 --> 00:07:04,480
you often read that Uber uses a
Kalman filter. In this article,

129
00:07:04,530 --> 00:07:07,230
the engineers explain
why they didn't go with it

130
00:07:07,350 --> 00:07:10,560
for this problem. Kalman assumes
a bell-shaped error,

131
00:07:10,610 --> 00:07:14,120
with a single peak. In a city,
the possible location has several.

132
00:07:14,850 --> 00:07:18,740
Even when accurate, the location arrives
in bursts. Signal cuts out under a bridge,

133
00:07:18,790 --> 00:07:21,580
the message shows up late.
If the app just displayed

134
00:07:21,630 --> 00:07:25,460
the last location received, the car
would teleport at any moment.

135
00:07:25,510 --> 00:07:27,800
Kind of like a teammate
lagging mid-hunt in Monster Hunter,

136
00:07:27,850 --> 00:07:31,520
who pops back up three meters away…
right under the Rathalos's claws.

137
00:07:32,130 --> 00:07:34,840
Uber hasn't detailed how
the app animates its cars.

138
00:07:34,890 --> 00:07:37,380
But online video games
use an old principle:

139
00:07:37,430 --> 00:07:40,900
between two positions, you slide
the car. And if the next one is late,

140
00:07:40,950 --> 00:07:45,020
you guess where it should be,
using its last speed and heading.

141
00:07:45,110 --> 00:07:48,980
That's called dead reckoning.

142
00:07:49,030 --> 00:07:53,540
Sailors did that before GPS existed.
That brings us back to the first question.

143
00:07:55,330 --> 00:07:59,880
In July 2015, two researchers,
Alex Rosenblat and Luke Stark,

144
00:07:59,930 --> 00:08:02,380
publish an investigation
into Uber drivers.

145
00:08:02,430 --> 00:08:04,580
They tell the story of a driver

146
00:08:04,630 --> 00:08:08,020
who opens the app from
the rider side. On the map,

147
00:08:08,070 --> 00:08:10,640
four cars right next
to her pickup point…

148
00:08:10,690 --> 00:08:12,880
and a seventeen-minute wait
for the closest one.

149
00:08:12,930 --> 00:08:16,380
Uber support tells her the map
is more of a "visual effect".

150
00:08:16,490 --> 00:08:19,550
And even that it's better to see it
as a "screen saver".

151
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:22,890
Officially, Uber denies this
story. According to them, the goal

152
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:26,230
is cars as accurate
as possible, and latency can

153
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:29,570
throw them off. And the app supposedly
only shows the closest eight:

154
00:08:29,620 --> 00:08:32,750
so there'd actually be
more cars than you see.

155
00:08:33,160 --> 00:08:36,010
On forums, some drivers
had their own explanation:

156
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:40,290
a 30 to 60 second lag. That lag,
you now know where it can come from.

157
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:41,830
It comes from late messages,

158
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:44,870
and an app that slides
the cars around by guessing.

159
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:48,470
But other drivers saw
ghosts wherever the rider dropped

160
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:51,610
their pin. That, though,
no lag can explain.

161
00:08:51,660 --> 00:08:54,590
That investigation is from 2015,
and the app has changed a lot since.

162
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:58,410
But the car sliding on the map?
Now you know what it is.

163
00:08:58,460 --> 00:09:00,930
At best, it's where a
driver was a few seconds ago,

164
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:02,010
partly guessed.

165
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:08,430
OK, let's recap the trip.
Our driver's phone sends

166
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:11,710
a location that can be off
by 50 meters. Uber files it

167
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:15,260
into a hexagon. Fireball decides
it's worth bothering you. RAMEN

168
00:09:15,350 --> 00:09:19,490
delivers it, and your app slides it
so you never see the gaps.

169
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:22,690
All that for something
you do in two seconds.

170
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:26,410
So, honestly, my buddy
was right. Showing stuff

171
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:29,370
on a map, I've done it
myself in my apps.

172
00:09:29,720 --> 00:09:33,130
Everything you just saw is what
it takes on top of that when millions

173
00:09:33,180 --> 00:09:38,470
of people are watching at once. So
yes, Uber's engineers are geniuses.

174
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:42,210
If you liked this video,
subscribe,

175
00:09:42,260 --> 00:09:45,290
leave a like and a comment.
See ya!
